  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 AM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Unseasonably warm and dry through much of the work \nweek, with record highs possible on Wednesday and perhaps Thursday.\n\nAn upper level ridge will strengthen and shift ewd through the week, \nwith the ridge centered over the Southeast US by Thursday. Surface \nhigh pressure will generally sit over the Southeast US through the \nweek. Meanwhile, a trough will strengthen over the mid-Atlantic/NC \nas low pressure tracks ewd from the Great Lakes to the Canadian \nMaritimes through Wednesday. A backdoor front may approach central \nNC from the northeast Wed night/Thu as Canadian high pressure trails \nthe aforementioned low, but still appears will stay generally north \nof the NC/VA border. The front should quickly lift away from the \narea as a rapidly deepening area of low pressure moves across the OH \nValley and Northeast Thu night/Fri. 12Z low-level thicknesses today \nshould be in the 1390-1400 dam range, increasing into the 1400-1410 \ndam range on Wed and Thu and possibly Fri. These thickness values \nwill support highs in the mid 80s today, increasing to mid 80s to \nlow 90s on Wed (about 15-20 degrees above normal), which would \nthreaten/break record highs on Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong cold front will sweep across the area \nFriday with a significant cooldown for the weekend and the potential \nfor some patchy frost.\n\nA potent nrn stream s/w aloft tracking ewd across wrn and cntl \nCanada Wed/Wed night will drop sewd across Ontario and the Great \nLakes Thu and Fri, deepening the trough over the ern US as it \ninteracts with another s/w ejecting enewd from the cntl Rockies. The \ntrough will continue to deepen as it moves ewd across the region and \noffshore Fri/Fri night. At the surface, a rapidly deepening area of \nlow pressure will lift newd across the OH Valley and Northeast Thu \nnight/Fri, the trailing cold front moving across central NC late \nFri/Fri night. The medium-range guidance still varies wrt precip \nchances with the fropa, the EC being the wetter solution but \ntrending drier. The pressure gradient will be quite stout Fri/Sat as \nthe low rapidly deepens near New England and strong ~1035 mb high \npressure builds in from the west. The low-level jet is also expected \nto strengthen over the area Fri eve. Expect strong, gusty winds with \nand behind the front Fri aft through Fri night. The high may weaken \never so slightly as it builds ewd across the mid-MS and OH Valley \nregions, before settling over the mid-Atlantic Sat night/Sun and \noffshore by Mon.\n\nBy Saturday, highs will be similar to the lows from Thu night \n(generally low to mid 60s), while lows may drop some 10-15 degrees \nfrom Fri morn to Sat morn. However, the more significant drop in \ntemps will come Sat night, as the high settles over the mid-\nAtlantic, when lows may drop into the mid/upr 30s across the nrn \nPiedmont and upper 30s to mid 40s elsewhere across cntl NC. Some \npatchy frost will be possible should the mid 30s lows be realized.",
